(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-7-cms-exam-7-master-exam-form-e"]={"title":"CMS I Exam 7 Master Exam — Form E","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 7","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 71-year-old man with kidney failure has vomited for 3 days. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.39 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 39 mm Hg (normal 35 to 45 mm Hg), bicarbonate 23 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L); sodium 138 mEq/L (normal 135 to 145 mEq/L), chloride 92 mEq/L (normal 98 to 106 mEq/L). Using 10 as the normal anion gap, what is his change in anion gap?","choices":["2","13","23","33"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Gap = 138 - (92 + 23) = 23. Change in gap = 23 - 10 = 13. Against a change in bicarbonate of only 25 - 23 = 2, the gap rose far more, the sign of a metabolic alkalosis from vomiting on top of the uremic acidosis. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 50, 27, 30"},{"q":"A 56-year-old man is seen for evaluation before elective knee surgery and has no known sleep disorder. His wife says he snores loudly enough to be heard through closed doors and has seen him stop breathing during sleep. He denies daytime tiredness. He is treated for high blood pressure. His body mass index is 33 kg/m2 and his neck (shirt collar) measures 16.5 inches. What is his STOP-Bang (snoring, tiredness, observed apnea, pressure, body mass index, age, neck, gender) score?","choices":["Score of 4","Score of 5","Score of 6","Score of 7"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Snoring 1 + tired 0 + observed apnea 1 + treated pressure 1 + body mass index 33 (not more than 35) 0 + age 56 (older than 50) 1 + neck 16.5 inches (16 or larger) 1 + male 1 = 6. A score of 5 to 8 places him in the high-risk group for obstructive sleep apnea before his surgery. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 16, 17"},{"q":"A 44-year-old woman has interstitial lung disease with a restrictive pattern. She has no occupational exposures, takes no medicines and has no hilar adenopathy. Her clinician wants a laboratory test that screens for a collagen vascular disease as the cause. Which test fits?","choices":["Antinuclear antibody","Anti-glomerular basement membrane antibody","Angiotensin-converting enzyme level","Cytoplasmic antineutrophil cytoplasmic antibody"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A high antinuclear antibody points to the collagen vascular diseases, the connective tissue disorders such as lupus, rheumatoid arthritis, Sjögren's syndrome and systemic sclerosis that produce an inflammatory, fibrotic interstitial response. With no exposures or drugs, an autoimmune cause is the next to consider. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 18, 25, 44"},{"q":"Carbon dioxide rises from 40 to 70 mm Hg within the past hour, from a baseline bicarbonate of 24 mEq/L. The acute bicarbonate rise is 1 mEq/L per 10 mm Hg and the chronic rise is 3.5 mEq/L per 10 mm Hg. What bicarbonate is expected?","choices":["24 mEq/L","27 mEq/L","30 mEq/L","34.5 mEq/L"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A rise within the past hour is acute, so bicarbonate climbs 1 mEq/L for each 10 mm Hg. Carbon dioxide rose 30 mm Hg, three steps of 10, so bicarbonate rises 3: 24 + 3 = 27 mEq/L. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 24, 33, 34, 35"},{"q":"A 67-year-old man with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease has had nasogastric suction for 3 days after bowel surgery. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.41 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 60 mm Hg (normal 35 to 45 mm Hg), bicarbonate 37 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). From a baseline carbon dioxide of 40 mm Hg and bicarbonate of 25 mEq/L, in chronic respiratory acidosis bicarbonate rises 3.5 mEq/L per 10 mm Hg rise in carbon dioxide. What does his bicarbonate indicate?","choices":["Appropriate acute compensation","A concurrent metabolic acidosis","A concurrent metabolic alkalosis","Appropriate chronic compensation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Chronic compensation predicts bicarbonate = 25 + 3.5 x (60 - 40) / 10 = 25 + 7 = 32. His is 37, 5 above prediction, so something beyond the kidneys is adding bicarbonate: loss of gastric acid by suction. A normal pH with a high carbon dioxide also signals a mixed disorder. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 23, 29, 34, 54"},{"q":"A 48-year-old man has worked as a sandblaster for 20 years. He has dyspnea, cough, weight loss and fatigue with crackles on examination. His chest film shows nodular opacities, 1-10 mm across, throughout both lungs, and eggshell calcification of the periphery of the hilar lymph nodes. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Sarcoidosis","Silicosis","Hypersensitivity pneumonitis","Asbestosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Sandblasting is a free crystalline silica exposure, and silicosis shows silicotic nodules 1-10 mm across throughout the lungs with eggshell calcification of the periphery of the hilar lymph nodes. Dyspnea, cough, weight loss, fatigue and crackles fit its presentation. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 26, 44, 58, 60, 61, 63, 69"},{"q":"A 45-year-old woman who has never smoked and has no family history of lung cancer has a solitary 4-mm solid nodule found on computed tomography done for abdominal pain. She asks whether she should be worried about it. Which response is accurate?","choices":["Its small size is reassuring","It needs a biopsy now","Size does not change concern","Most nodules are cancerous"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The first evaluation of a nodule is its size, and anything smaller than 6 mm is generally not concerning unless the patient is at high risk. Most nodules are benign, mainly granulomas or hamartomas. She has no smoking or family history, so her 4-mm nodule falls in the low-concern group. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 12, 16"},{"q":"A 72-year-old man has chronic kidney failure that has worsened over months, adrenal insufficiency on hormone replacement, glaucoma treated with an oral carbonic anhydrase inhibitor, and leg edema treated with a loop diuretic. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.32 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 30 mm Hg (normal 35 to 45 mm Hg), bicarbonate 15 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). Serum sodium 138 mEq/L (normal 135 to 145 mEq/L), chloride 103 mEq/L (normal 98 to 106 mEq/L), bicarbonate 15 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). Which part of his history best accounts for this blood gas?","choices":["The loop diuretic","The carbonic anhydrase inhibitor","His adrenal insufficiency","His kidney failure"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Low pH and bicarbonate make a metabolic acidosis, and the gap is 138 - (15 + 103) = 20, above 12. Renal failure, acute or chronic, is one of the big four raised-gap causes. His other conditions do not raise the gap. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 44, 45, 46"},{"q":"What does the Winter formula predict?","choices":["Carbon dioxide in metabolic acidosis","Carbon dioxide in metabolic alkalosis","Anion gap after albumin correction","Bicarbonate in respiratory acidosis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The Winter formula, expected carbon dioxide = 1.5 x bicarbonate + 8 plus or minus 2, predicts the respiratory compensation for a metabolic acidosis, raised gap or normal gap. Carbon dioxide above the range means a concurrent respiratory acidosis; below means a concurrent respiratory alkalosis. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 49, 24, 8"},{"q":"A 61-year-old woman received chest radiation for breast cancer 3 years ago. She now has interstitial lung disease with a restrictive pattern and has no occupational exposures. She asks whether her cancer treatment could be the cause. Which response is accurate?","choices":["No, cancer treatment spares the lungs","No, radiation affects the skin alone","Yes, by acting as an inhaled dust","Yes, radiation is a recognized cause"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Radiation is one of the treatment-related causes of interstitial lung disease of known cause, listed with methotrexate, amiodarone, nitrofurantoin and chemotherapeutics. It is also named among the causes that provoke an inflammatory and fibrotic lung response, so her chest radiation is a credible cause. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 12, 13, 17"},{"q":"A 52-year-old man's polysomnogram shows repeated events in which thermistor airflow falls by 90% or more for 15 seconds while chest and abdominal respiratory effort continue. How is each event classified?","choices":["Obstructive apnea","Mixed apnea","Hypopnea","Central apnea"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. An apnea is a 90% or greater fall in airflow on the thermistor lasting at least 10 seconds, and when airflow stops but respiratory effort continues, the apnea is obstructive. The airway collapses while the patient still tries to breathe, the event pattern of obstructive sleep apnea. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 3, 24"},{"q":"A 34-year-old woman lies in bed at lights-out with her eyes closed. Her electroencephalogram shows rhythmical waves at 8 to 13 cycles per second, strongest over the occipital region. Which state does this tracing represent?","choices":["Stage N1 sleep","Rapid eye movement sleep","Alert wakefulness","Quiet wakefulness"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Rhythmical waves at 8 to 13 cycles per second, strongest over the occipital region, are alpha waves, the mark of quiet wakefulness: an awake person with the eyes closed, relaxed, with a decreased level of alertness. She has not yet fallen asleep. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 22, 27, 28, 29, 9"},{"q":"In which stage of small cell carcinoma does radiation work best?","choices":["Extensive stage","Any stage equally","Combined type","Limited stage"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Small cell carcinoma is divided by metastasis into limited stage, confined to one area with only local lymph nodes involved, extensive stage, spread away from the local area, and combined. Radiation works best when the cancer is in limited stage. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 41, 43"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman in the intensive care unit has a subclavian vein catheter placed for venous access. An hour later she reports new right-sided pleuritic chest pain and dyspnea, and a chest radiograph shows a visceral pleural line on the right. She has no history of lung disease or chest trauma. How is her pneumothorax classified?","choices":["Traumatic","Primary spontaneous","Iatrogenic","Secondary spontaneous"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Iatrogenic pneumothorax is injury to the pleura secondary to a medical intervention, and subclavian vein catheter placement is one of the listed examples, with thoracentesis, bronchoscopy and mechanical ventilation. Her symptoms began an hour after the catheter, with no lung disease or trauma. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A 59-year-old man who smokes has a bronchial biopsy showing dysplasia: the cells have changed form but have not invaded the surrounding tissue. He asks what would come next if these cells keep progressing. Which change comes next?","choices":["Invasion of blood and lymph","Hyperplasia","In situ cancer","Distant metastases"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Lung cancer develops in a sequence: a genetically altered epithelial cell, hyperplasia (cells dividing faster than normal), dysplasia (cells changing form), in situ cancer (cells staying in one place), then a malignant tumor whose cells invade normal tissue and enter blood and lymph, and finally distant metastases. After dysplasia comes in situ cancer. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"What is the most common presenting symptom of interstitial lung disease?","choices":["Productive cough","Hemoptysis","Shortness of breath","Chest pain"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Fibrosis makes the lung tissue stiff and restrictive, no longer expanding as it once did, which makes breathing harder; patients most commonly present with shortness of breath, typically progressive exertional dyspnea, sometimes with a persistent nonproductive cough. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 4, 23"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man with a 3.5-cm non-small cell lung carcinoma, no lymph node involvement and no metastasis (stage 1B) is fit for surgery. He would prefer to avoid an operation and asks for chemotherapy and radiation instead. Which plan should be avoided as his primary treatment?","choices":["Chemoradiation without surgery","Wedge or segment resection","Preoperative lung function tests","Lobectomy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Stage 1 non-small cell lung cancer is treated with surgical resection when the patient can tolerate it, and chemotherapy and radiation serve only as adjuncts to surgery or for stage 4, because non-small cell cancers respond poorly to them. For a fit patient with stage 1B disease, replacing surgery with chemoradiation gives up the standard treatment. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 14, 35"},{"q":"A 2-month-old boy's electroencephalogram shows slow waves below 3.5 cycles per second. Compared with most other types of brain waves, how large is their voltage expected to be?","choices":["Much smaller","About the same","2 to 4 times greater","20 to 40 times greater"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Delta waves, which include all waves below 3.5 cycles per second and occur in infancy, often have voltages 2 to 4 times greater than most other types of brain waves. On a tracing they stand out as tall, slow waves, in contrast to the low-voltage beta waves of an alert adult. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 26, 21, 23"},{"q":"Which central lung cancer is usually metastatic by the time it is diagnosed?","choices":["Small cell carcinoma","Squamous cell carcinoma","Adenocarcinoma","Large cell carcinoma"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Small cell carcinoma is central, infiltrates the mucosa and develops insidiously, so over 70% of patients are already metastatic to the liver, bone or brain before detection. That is why it is usually metastatic by the time of diagnosis. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 30, 31, 33, 39, 40, 41"},{"q":"A 61-year-old man with hypertension and a 40-pack-year smoking history has sudden sharp left chest pain that worsens with deep breathing. Before the pain is attributed to pleurisy, which condition belongs among those that must be considered immediately?","choices":["Costochondritis","Herpes zoster nerve pain","Rib fracture","Aortic dissection"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Pleuritic pain demands immediate consideration of the dangerous causes: myocardial infarction, pericarditis, aortic dissection, pneumonia, pneumothorax and pulmonary embolism. Only after those are weighed are the other causes, such as rib fracture, costochondritis, vertebral fracture or zoster nerve pain, considered. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 74-year-old woman has a very large left pleural effusion, estimated at more than 1,000 mL on imaging, with marked dyspnea. Which inspection finding is expected?","choices":["Trachea pulled to the left","Symmetric chest expansion","Trachea shifted to the right","No findings at this size"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. With an effusion over 1,000 mL, the trachea shifts away from the involved side, so a left effusion pushes it to the right; inspection also shows asymmetrical thoracic expansion. Small effusions under 300 mL produce no examination findings, but hers is large. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slide 87"},{"q":"Which apex-to-cupola distance separates a small from a large pneumothorax in the American College of Chest Physicians system?","choices":["1 cm","2 cm","3 cm","5 cm"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The American College of Chest Physicians defines pneumothorax size by the apex-to-cupola distance, with a distance under 3 cm called small and larger distances called large. That one number then sorts the treatment of primary and secondary spontaneous pneumothorax. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 18, 28, 53, 55"},{"q":"A 34-year-old man on a mountain trek at high altitude wakes frequently at night. His tent partner notices that his breathing speeds up and then stops, over and over, without snoring. He has no heart failure and takes no medications. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Nocturnal asthma","Obstructive sleep apnea","Panic attacks","Central sleep apnea"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. High altitude is a risk factor for central sleep apnea, in which increased sensitivity to carbon dioxide produces an unstable pattern of hyperventilation alternating with apnea. Frequent awakenings without snoring fit, since snoring is less common than in obstructive sleep apnea. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 24, 25, 10"},{"q":"A 62-year-old woman with a new pleural effusion undergoes diagnostic thoracentesis. Pleural fluid protein is 3.9 g/dL with serum protein 6.5 g/dL (normal 6.0-8.3 g/dL). Pleural fluid lactate dehydrogenase is 150 units/L with serum lactate dehydrogenase 200 units/L (normal 140-280 units/L). How many of Light's criteria does the fluid meet, and what does that make it?","choices":["0 of 3; transudate","1 of 3; exudate","2 of 3; exudate","3 of 3; exudate"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Protein ratio 3.9 / 6.5 = 0.60, above 0.5: met. Lactate dehydrogenase ratio 150 / 200 = 0.75, above 0.6: met. Fluid lactate dehydrogenase 150 is under two-thirds of the 280 upper limit, about 187: not met. Two of three, and one is already enough for an exudate. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 101, 112"},{"q":"A 66-year-old retired construction worker has asbestosis with exertional dyspnea and fine bibasilar crackles. He asks about a procedure to wash the asbestos fibers out of his lungs. Which treatment plan fits?","choices":["Lavage to wash out the fibers","Methotrexate to stop the fibrosis","Corticosteroids to dissolve the fibers","Oxygen, physiotherapy and vaccinations"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Treatment of asbestosis is supportive because asbestos fibers invade lung tissue and cannot be removed. Oxygen therapy, physiotherapy, medications and vaccinations make up that care, while the scarring itself comes from chronic inflammation and macrophage invasion forming fibrosis. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 5, 18, 46, 56, 62"},{"q":"Which finding gives a definitive diagnosis of hypersensitivity pneumonitis?","choices":["Little change on acute imaging","Serum antibodies to the agent","Restrictive pulmonary function","White cell count with left shift"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Antibodies to the agent in serum give a definitive diagnosis of hypersensitivity pneumonitis. Supporting findings include an elevated white blood cell count with a left shift, hypoxia and restrictive pulmonary function, and lung biopsy may be indicated in subacute or chronic disease. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 29, 57, 69"},{"q":"A 54-year-old woman collapses 6 days after a cesarean delivery. She is pale and sweating, her heart rate is 128/min, her blood pressure is 78/48 mmHg and her neck veins are distended. Computed tomographic pulmonary angiography shows a large central pulmonary embolism, and echocardiography shows an acutely dilated right ventricle with the septum bowing toward the left ventricle. What best explains her hypotension?","choices":["Right ventricular wall hypertrophy","Reduced left ventricular filling","Left ventricular contractile failure","Widespread systemic vasodilation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A massive embolism spikes right ventricular wall tension, and the right ventricle dilates acutely. The septum bows leftward into the left ventricle, so left ventricular diastolic filling falls, output plummets and systemic hypotension follows. Her distended neck veins and septal shift trace that chain. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 9, 10, 34, 50"},{"q":"A 67-year-old man has a biopsy-proven 2.5-cm non-small cell carcinoma in the left upper lobe. Positron emission tomography shows uptake in mediastinal and subcarinal lymph nodes on the same side as the tumor, and there is no distant metastasis. Which stage is his cancer?","choices":["Stage 2B","Stage 3A","Stage 3B","Stage 4A"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Mediastinal or subcarinal node involvement (N2) automatically moves a cancer to stage 3, and a tumor no more than 5 cm that has spread to the mediastinal and subcarinal nodes is stage 3A. His tumor is only 2.5 cm, but the same-side mediastinal nodes set the stage. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 22, 25, 26, 27, 29"},{"q":"A 55-year-old woman with cirrhosis takes a diuretic, is anxious and has low albumin. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.58 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 34 mm Hg (normal 35 to 45 mm Hg), bicarbonate 31 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L); sodium 135 mEq/L (normal 135 to 145 mEq/L), chloride 93 mEq/L (normal 98 to 106 mEq/L). From a baseline carbon dioxide of 40 mm Hg and bicarbonate of 25 mEq/L, in metabolic alkalosis carbon dioxide rises 0.75 mm Hg per 1 mEq/L rise in bicarbonate. Which part of her history best explains the metabolic alkalosis?","choices":["Her diuretic","Her liver disease alone","Her low albumin","Her anxiety"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Bicarbonate 31 with pH 7.58 is a metabolic alkalosis; expected carbon dioxide = 40 + 0.75 x 6 = 44.5, but hers is 34, so a respiratory alkalosis is also present. Diuretics are a saline-responsive cause of metabolic alkalosis, and liver disease with diuretics is the listed setting. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 29, 53, 39"},{"q":"Which activity advice fits an adult with pulmonary hypertension?","choices":["Unsupervised sprint training","Supervised aerobic rehabilitation","Strict bedrest at home","Rest until fatigue resolves"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Strict bedrest is avoided: regular, steady aerobic exercise in a supervised rehabilitation program is safe, prevents muscle deconditioning and improves fatigue. It sits alongside careful diuresis, oxygen during sleep or exertion and annual influenza and pneumococcal immunization. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 63-year-old woman with pulmonary arterial hypertension has ankle edema, ascites and a right ventricular S3 gallop. Her symptoms have worsened rapidly over 3 months, she has fainted several times, she is short of breath at rest (World Health Organization functional class IV), and her six-minute walk distance is 140 m. Which prognostic risk band fits her?","choices":["Low risk, under 5% at 1 year","Intermediate risk, 5-10% at 1 year","High risk, 5-10% at 1 year","High risk, over 10% at 1 year"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Signs of right ventricular failure (edema, ascites, a right ventricular S3) place her in the high-risk row, and every other feature agrees: rapid progression, repeated syncope, class IV and a walk under 165 m. High risk carries an estimated 1-year mortality above 10%. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 22, 25"},{"q":"With 5 or more predominantly obstructive respiratory events per hour of sleep, what else confirms obstructive sleep apnea?","choices":["Serum bicarbonate above 27","A high-risk questionnaire score","Body mass index above 30","A qualifying symptom"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. At 5 or more events per hour, one qualifying symptom completes the diagnosis: sleepiness, fatigue or insomnia impairing quality of life; waking with breath holding, gasping or choking; or habitual snoring or breathing interruptions noted by an observer. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 9, 16, 32"},{"q":"A 67-year-old woman smoked 2 packs a day for 20 years and quit 8 years ago. She has no cough, hemoptysis or weight loss, and no lung nodule has ever been found. She asks about lung cancer screening. Which is the most appropriate recommendation?","choices":["Annual low-dose computed tomography","Yearly sputum cytology","No screening, since she quit","Yearly standard-dose computed tomography"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Screening is recommended for high-risk adults without symptoms or a known nodule who are 55 to 80 years old, with at least a 30 pack-year history, who smoke now or quit within the last 15 years. She is 67, has 2 packs a day x 20 years = 40 pack-years, and quit 8 years ago, so she qualifies for an annual low-dose chest scan. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 3, 14, 18"},{"q":"A 34-year-old man is brought in after a high-speed motor vehicle crash. He is in severe respiratory distress, with blood pressure 78/50 mm Hg and heart rate 136 beats per minute. His trachea is deviated to the left, his neck veins are distended, his right chest is hyperresonant and breath sounds are absent on the right. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Hemothorax","Open pneumothorax","Tension pneumothorax","Pneumomediastinum"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Air enters the pleural space but cannot escape, a one-way valve, causing severe ventilatory compromise and a mediastinal shift to the contralateral side. Decreased venous return and cardiac output follow, with hypotension, tracheal deviation, neck vein distention and absent breath sounds, his exact picture. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 62, 63, 64, 66, 77, 88"},{"q":"An 84-year-old woman with heart failure with preserved ejection fraction is admitted with acute pulmonary edema. Her blood pressure is 148/82 mmHg and her creatinine is 1.1 mg/dL (normal 0.6-1.1). Echocardiography shows a stiff, thick left ventricle with a high end-diastolic pressure. Which diuresis strategy fits her?","choices":["Withholding diuretics entirely","Gentle, precise diuresis","Aggressive, rapid diuresis","Morphine before any diuretic"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Elderly patients usually have heart failure with preserved ejection fraction from age-related myocardial stiffening, with a high end-diastolic pressure and extreme sensitivity to preload. Their therapeutic margin is narrow, so diuresis must be gentle and precise. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 39, 42"},{"q":"Needle decompression of a tension pneumothorax is also known as what?","choices":["Tube thoracostomy","Needle thoracostomy","Catheter aspiration","Thoracentesis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Needle decompression, also known as needle thoracostomy, is insertion of a needle into the pleural space to decompress a tension pneumothorax, typically in the 2nd intercostal space, over the 3rd rib, at the midclavicular line. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 38, 39, 41, 67, 96"},{"q":"A 51-year-old woman has sudden dyspnea and a swollen, tender left leg, and her Wells score is 6. She had anaphylaxis to iodinated contrast during a previous scan. Her creatinine is 0.7 mg/dL (normal 0.6-1.1), and she has no chronic lung disease. Which test should confirm or exclude pulmonary embolism?","choices":["A high-sensitivity D-dimer","Computed tomographic pulmonary angiography","Bedside echocardiography","Ventilation-perfusion scan"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Severe contrast allergy is one of the reasons to choose the ventilation-perfusion scan over computed tomographic pulmonary angiography. With no chronic lung disease the scan is less likely to be nondiagnostic, and a completely negative scan excludes embolism with the same certainty as a negative pulmonary angiogram. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 55, 56, 57"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man with heart failure, poor tissue perfusion and a low albumin is admitted. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.32 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 32 mm Hg (normal 35 to 45 mm Hg), bicarbonate 16 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). Serum sodium 140 mEq/L (normal 135 to 145 mEq/L), chloride 108 mEq/L (normal 98 to 106 mEq/L), bicarbonate 16 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). His albumin is 2.9 g/dL (normal 3.5 to 5.5 g/dL). What is his anion gap after correction for his albumin?","choices":["16","17.5","20","22.5"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The measured gap is 140 - (16 + 108) = 16. His albumin is 4.5 - 2.9 = 1.6 g/dL below 4.5, and 1.6 x 2.5 = 4, so the corrected gap is 16 + 4 = 20, a raised gap that fits lactic acidosis from poor perfusion. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 8, 45, 57"},{"q":"A 44-year-old man has a small pulmonary embolism on computed tomographic pulmonary angiography, with a normal right ventricle. His blood pressure is 132/84 mmHg, his troponin I is under 0.01 ng/mL (normal under 0.04), and his B-type natriuretic peptide is 40 pg/mL (normal under 100). How is his embolism risk-stratified, and what follows for treatment?","choices":["Low risk; standard anticoagulation","Low risk; no treatment needed","Massive; systemic thrombolysis","Submassive; anticoagulate, monitor"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Normotension, no right ventricular dysfunction and negative cardiac biomarkers define low-risk embolism. It carries an excellent prognosis and is treated with standard anticoagulation, which halts clot propagation while the body's own fibrinolysis works. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 58, 59"},{"q":"A 78-year-old woman with known heart failure has increasing lower extremity edema, orthopnea and paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnea, and her chest radiograph shows bilateral pleural effusions. She is comfortable at rest. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Diuretic therapy","Chemical pleurodesis","Diagnostic thoracentesis","Indwelling pleural catheter"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Her edema, orthopnea and paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnea give the effusions a clear clinical cause: heart failure, which raises capillary hydrostatic pressure and causes more than 90% of transudates. Effusions are managed by treating the underlying disorder, and diuretics are the initial treatment for heart failure transudates. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 82, 86, 96, 97, 111, 113"},{"q":"A 30-year-old resident falls asleep after a 30-hour shift without any sleep. What is expected of the rapid eye movement episodes on her recording tonight?","choices":["Short or even absent","The same as on rested nights","More frequent than usual","Longer than on rested nights"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. When a person is extremely sleepy, each rapid eye movement episode is short or even absent; as the person becomes more rested, the episodes lengthen. After 30 hours awake she is extremely sleepy, so tonight her rapid eye movement episodes should be brief or missing. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 9, 6"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man who has smoked for 50 years has 3 days of worsening dyspnea and wheezing. His chest film shows hyperinflation without vascular congestion, his arterial carbon dioxide is 61 mmHg (normal 35-45), and his B-type natriuretic peptide is 70 pg/mL (normal under 100). What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Acute cardiogenic pulmonary edema","Heart failure exacerbation","Acute pulmonary embolism","Obstructive lung disease flare"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Wheezing, hyperinflation on the chest film and hypercapnia point to an exacerbation of chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, and the natriuretic peptide is usually normal in that setting, as his is. Dyspnea, hypoxemia and tachycardia are shared with heart failure and embolism, so these features decide. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"A 30-year-old woman is scheduled for pleurodesis after her third spontaneous pneumothorax. She asks what the procedure will do inside her chest. Which explanation is accurate?","choices":["Drains air through a valve","Makes the pleural layers adhere","Staples ruptured blebs shut","Removes the visceral pleura"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Pleurodesis obliterates the pleural space by inducing inflammation there: an irritant causes adhesions of the two layers of pleura, which helps keep the lung inflated after pneumothorax and leaves no room for fluid in an effusion. It is used to prevent recurrent or treat resistant pneumothorax. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 40, 45, 46, 47"},{"q":"A 47-year-old woman has months of episodes of skin flushing, watery diarrhea with cramps, and wheezing. Exam shows an enlarged liver and a heart murmur, and echocardiography shows thickening of the tricuspid and pulmonic valves. Computed tomography shows a mass in a bronchus. Which tumor is most likely?","choices":["Small cell carcinoma","Squamous cell carcinoma","Adenocarcinoma","Bronchial carcinoid"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Carcinoid syndrome, which in the lung arises mainly from a bronchial carcinoid, comes from release of serotonin by the tumor cells. Its features include cutaneous flushes, diarrhea, cramps, nausea and vomiting, cough, wheezing and dyspnea, hepatomegaly, and pulmonic and tricuspid valve thickening and stenosis, matching her picture. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 4, 31, 46, 48"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man who smokes 2 packs a day is worried after his brother was diagnosed with lung cancer. He asks which type of lung cancer is most strongly associated with smoking. Which response is accurate?","choices":["Large cell carcinoma","Adenocarcinoma","Squamous cell carcinoma","Bronchial carcinoid"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Squamous cell carcinoma, the second most common lung cancer, has the strongest association with smoking. Smoking is implicated in 85 to 90% of all lung cancers and compounds with packs per day and years, but among the cell types it is squamous cell carcinoma that is singled out as most strongly tied to smoking. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 3, 4, 31, 33, 39, 48"},{"q":"What does the S1Q3T3 pattern on the electrocardiogram suggest?","choices":["Severe right ventricular strain","Left ventricular hypertrophy","Right atrial enlargement","Pulmonary embolism specifically"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The S1Q3T3 pattern, a deep S wave in lead I with a Q wave and an inverted T wave in lead III, suggests severe right ventricular strain, not purely pulmonary embolism. It is highly specific but has low sensitivity, so its absence does not exclude embolism. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 51, 14"},{"q":"A 20-year-old student plans to sleep 3 hours a night for the 4 nights before an exam, reasoning that nobody knows what sleep is for anyway. What should he be told?","choices":["Sleep's role is unknown, so cutting it is safe","Short nights can impair his thinking","Short sleep mainly costs physical strength","Harm needs months of short sleep"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Sleep restriction over a few days can decrease cognitive and physical performance, productivity and health, and the functions of sleep include cognition and the facilitation of learning or memory. Four nights of 3 hours is restriction over a few days, so his studying could backfire. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 16, 17"},{"q":"A 52-year-old man is sedated on a mechanical ventilator after abdominal surgery. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.55 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 26 mm Hg (normal 35 to 45 mm Hg), bicarbonate 22 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). Which ventilator change is most appropriate?","choices":["Lower the set rate or tidal volume","Give saline with potassium replacement","Leave the settings; mild alkalosis needs nothing","Raise the set rate or tidal volume"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A pH of 7.55 with a carbon dioxide of 26 is a respiratory alkalosis, and on a ventilator the fix is to decrease minute volume by lowering the number of breaths per minute or the tidal volume, so less carbon dioxide is blown off. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 38, 40"},{"q":"In the proposed theory, what ends a long period of wakefulness?","choices":["Rising parasympathetic tone","Fatigue of activating neurons","Stronger positive feedback","Release of activating nuclei"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. After the brain has remained active for many hours, neurons in the activating system become fatigued. The positive feedback cycle between the mesencephalic reticular nuclei and the cerebral cortex fades, and the sleep-promoting centers take over. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 14, 18, 19"},{"q":"A 25-year-old man presents with his second primary spontaneous pneumothorax on the right; the first was treated 18 months ago. He is stable. Which management best prevents further recurrence?","choices":["Catheter aspiration, then discharge","Observation with 100% oxygen","Thoracoscopic surgery with pleurodesis","Chest tube, then discharge"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A recurrent primary spontaneous pneumothorax calls for surgical therapy, video-assisted thoracoscopic surgery with pleurodesis, which is the best preventive procedure. Recurrence is common, approaching 50% within three years after a first episode, and his second episode proves the risk. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 39, 53, 55, 59"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man with obstructive sleep apnea has not been helped by continuous positive airway pressure or an oral appliance. He asks which operation would be a cure. Which procedure is described as a cure for obstructive sleep apnea?","choices":["Tracheostomy","Bariatric surgery","Radiofrequency ablation","Palatal implants"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Tracheostomy is a cure for obstructive sleep apnea because the site of upper airway obstruction is bypassed. Breathing no longer depends on the narrowed upper airway that collapses during sleep. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 63-year-old woman with a 35 pack-year history has a 9-mm solid nodule in the right upper lobe found on computed tomography for abdominal pain. Which additional feature of the nodule would most raise concern for malignancy?","choices":["Central calcification","Spiculated border","Popcorn calcification","Internal fat"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Concerning nodules are greater than 6 mm, sit in the upper lobes or hilar structures, have a spiculated appearance (spikes, deviations or lobulations in the border), number more than 3, enlarge over time, enhance with contrast, appear cavitated or nonsolid, or occur in high-risk patients. A spiky border would add to her size, location and smoking. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 16, 21"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man has 3 days of mild pleuritic chest pain. His Wells score is 1.5 (heart rate 104/min). His oxygen saturation is 97%, and he has no hemoptysis, no estrogen use, no prior clots, no recent surgery or trauma and no leg swelling. Because he fails the PERC (Pulmonary Embolism Rule-out Criteria), a D-dimer is sent, and it is 310 ng/mL (normal under 500 ng/mL). Which step fits?","choices":["Computed tomographic angiography","Repeat the D-dimer in 1 week","Ventilation-perfusion scan","No imaging; embolism is excluded"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. He fails two criteria (age 58 and heart rate 104/min), so a D-dimer was right with a Wells score of 4 or less. The D-dimer has high sensitivity, and a negative result safely excludes embolism in this low-probability outpatient, so no imaging is needed. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 53, 54, 57"},{"q":"A 41-year-old woman with sarcoidosis has a chest film showing diffuse reticular infiltrates throughout both lungs. Her previously enlarged hilar nodes are no longer seen, and there is no honeycombing or hilar retraction. Which radiographic stage is this?","choices":["Stage I","Stage II","Stage III","Stage IV"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Stage III is parenchymal involvement alone, seen as diffuse reticular infiltrates, with no hilar adenopathy. Her film has infiltrates without enlarged nodes and without the honeycombing, hilar retraction, bullae or cysts that would mark stage IV. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man is brought in confused with a low blood pressure, and the cause of his critical illness is not yet clear. Which initial testing best characterizes his acid-base status?","choices":["Urine chloride before any blood testing","Arterial gas and electrolytes drawn together","Electrolytes alone, adding a gas if abnormal","Arterial gas now, electrolytes the next day"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The first step is to obtain an arterial blood gas and electrolytes simultaneously, so the bicarbonate on each can be compared to verify accuracy and the anion gap can be calculated. In a critically ill patient the acid-base type can assist the diagnosis. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 9, 26, 56"},{"q":"A 71-year-old woman with cor pulmonale from interstitial lung disease has ankle edema and mild ascites. Her blood pressure is 112/70 mmHg and her potassium is 4.1 mEq/L (normal 3.5-5.0). A loop diuretic is started. Which approach to her diuresis should be avoided?","choices":["Adding spironolactone to the loop","Checking potassium during diuresis","Careful, monitored diuresis","Aggressive, rapid volume removal"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The damaged right ventricle relies on elevated filling pressure (preload) to keep output up. Removing volume too aggressively drops that preload and starves the left ventricle, and output can fall catastrophically. Careful, monitored diuresis treats her edema without crashing her output. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 16, 27"},{"q":"A 72-year-old man with no occupational, drug or connective tissue disease history has 2 years of insidious dry cough and exertional dyspnea. He has bibasilar inspiratory crackles, pulmonary function testing shows a restrictive pattern, chest films over several years show progressive fibrosis, and high-resolution computed tomography shows diffuse, patchy fibrosis with pleural-based honeycombing. How should the diagnosis be established?","choices":["Clinically, without a lung biopsy","By surgical lung biopsy","By transbronchial lung biopsy","By serum antibody testing"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Over age 65, idiopathic disease with inspiratory crackles, restrictive physiology, radiographic progressive fibrosis over several years and diffuse, patchy fibrosis with pleural-based honeycombing on high-resolution computed tomography give idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis with 90% confidence. Such patients do not need surgical lung biopsy. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 32, 33, 34, 35, 44, 69"},{"q":"A 19-year-old man is resuscitated after a near-drowning. Within 24 hours he has refractory hypoxemia and new bilateral opacities on chest film with a normal heart size, and an echocardiogram shows normal cardiac function. On the ventilator his arterial oxygen pressure is 54 mm Hg on a fraction of inspired oxygen of 0.60. How is his oxygenation impairment classified?","choices":["Oxygenation criterion not met","Mild acute respiratory distress syndrome","Moderate acute respiratory distress syndrome","Severe acute respiratory distress syndrome"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Divide arterial oxygen pressure by fraction of inspired oxygen: 54 / 0.60 = 90 mm Hg. A ratio under 100 is the severe class. Near-drowning is a direct pulmonary insult, and new bilateral opacities within a day with a normal heart complete the criteria. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 72, 73"},{"q":"A 57-year-old coal miner with complicated coal workers' pneumoconiosis has progressive dyspnea. Which pulmonary function pattern, including the diffusing capacity of the lungs for carbon monoxide, is expected?","choices":["Normal volumes, high diffusing capacity","Restriction with low diffusing capacity","Obstruction with high diffusing capacity","Restriction with high diffusing capacity"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Coal workers' pneumoconiosis produces restrictive dysfunction with a reduced diffusing capacity of the lungs for carbon monoxide. Simple disease can leave testing unimpressive, but his complicated, symptomatic disease brings the restrictive pattern and impaired gas transfer. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 51, 53"},{"q":"A 33-year-old woman with self-induced vomiting has a metabolic alkalosis. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.51 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 47 mm Hg (normal 35 to 45 mm Hg), bicarbonate 36 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). Her potassium is 2.9 mEq/L (normal 3.5 to 5.0 mEq/L). Her vomiting has stopped and saline has been started. Which approach to her potassium should be avoided?","choices":["Rechecking potassium as the alkalosis corrects","Treating the cause of her vomiting","Replacing well beyond the measured deficit","Replacing potassium alongside her fluids"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Much of the low potassium in alkalosis is a shift into cells. Potassium deficits are treated if needed, but overcorrection is to be avoided, because as the alkalosis corrects potassium shifts back out of the cells. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 5, 54"}]};
